From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Jun 2026 19:25:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03333/11362] [outer-loop] arc: CORRECT the gate verdict --
 goal-induction was a corpus artifact; real gap is exploration-to-first-win

A 3-agent live-process investigation overturned this session's 'goal-induction is the bottleneck'
verdict -- it was a static-corpus artifact (the exact trap the new CLAUDE.md live-discovery-agent
rule warns about). Win-DETECTION is NOT the gap: the live solver reads frame.levels_completed from
the env (already reproduces 52 levels); the offline n_win_states=0/0-of-4 came purely from the
corpus never containing a level-up (lb=la=0, dropped at the shard-build stage; the signal is in the
raw parquet). The REAL gap is EXPLORATION-TO-FIRST-WIN: StepwiseExplorer does undirected BFS with no
bias toward the latent win-mechanic, so on unseen games it never triggers the first level-up or
burns the ~5n budget -- the 0.08 wall. Highest-EV lever (pure PROCESS, no weights, transfers by
construction): a goal-biased online explorer that induces win-predicates from frame-deltas, confirms
them against the env level signal, and uses them as the frontier priority key. Still NOT gate-ready;
the lever is now correctly identified. First payoff of the live-discovery-agent framing rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
